  "raw_meta_yaml": "# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/prefix-dev/recipe-format/main/schema.json\nschema_version: 1\n\ncontext:\n  version: \"5.0.7\"\n\npackage:\n  name: node-red\n  version: ${{ version }}\n\nsource:\n  - url: https://registry.npmjs.org/node-red/-/node-red-${{ version }}.tgz\n    sha256: e701362fda8930bba62a138276f147c21fcbeb61fb9778d6d130467f6d02e753\n    patches:\n      # conda-forge nodejs builds can report a prerelease tag in\n      # process.version (e.g. v26.8.0-alpha.0.0.0). semver.satisfies() rejects\n      # any prerelease against a non-prerelease range, so node-red refuses to\n      # start. Pass includePrerelease so the >=22.9.0 floor is honoured without\n      # tripping over the tag.\n      - patches/0001-accept-prerelease-node-versions.patch\n\nbuild:\n  number: 0\n  script: build.nu\n  # noarch: generic # has binaries on macos, so not 'noarch'\n\nrequirements:\n  build:\n    - nodejs\n    # pnpm 12 replaced the JS entry point with a native binary, and the\n    # conda-forge package ships the x86_64 build on every subdir. On\n    # linux-aarch64 that fails as \"Exec format error (os error 8)\" the moment\n    # `pnpm install` is spawned; osx-arm64 only survives it via Rosetta. pnpm 11\n    # is still bin/pnpm.cjs, a plain Node script, so it runs anywhere.\n    # Drop this pin once pnpm-feedstock ships per-platform binaries.\n    - pnpm <12\n    - pnpm-licenses\n    - nushell\n  run:\n    - nodejs\n\ntests:\n  # Test 1: Verify package contents are included\n  - package_contents:\n      files:\n        - if: not win\n          then:\n            - share/node-red/service.yaml\n          else:\n            - Library\\share\\node-red\\service.yaml\n  # Test 2: Functional tests\n  - script:\n      content:\n        - node-red --help\n\nabout:\n  homepage: https://nodered.org\n  summary: Low-code programming for event-driven applications\n  description: |\n    Node-RED's goal is to enable anyone to build applications that collect,\n      transform and visualize their data;\n      building flows that can automate their world.\n      Its low-code nature makes it accessible to users of any background,\n      whether for home automation,\n      industrial control systems or anything in between.\n  license: Apache-2.0\n  license_file:\n    - LICENSE\n    # Generated by pnpm-licenses in build.nu; covers the ~280 bundled npm deps.\n    - third-party-licenses.txt\n  documentation: https://nodered.org/docs/\n  repository: https://github.com/node-red/node-red\n\nextra:\n  recipe-maintainers:\n    - phreed\n",
